u  can show them and the other school of  thought is show them show them show them  everything and i believe that both is  true i believe the audience can come up  with something more horrible in their  minds than i can show them provided  they're given the raw materials to  construct something  and i also believe that i have got some  pretty horrible things to show myself so  i try and mix and match i try and scare  them and  weird them out with visuals and i try  and leave room for them to add their own  ingredients to create one of the things  that we learned from  our repeated visits to the horror  picture  were certain laws that happen to apply  to all horror pictures  and that is  the innocent must suffer  the poor sap who walks into the house  has to be tormented  and then the next law came into uh  we came to realize there was a second  law governing all horror pictures and  that was the guilty must be punished  whatever evil thing there is out there  whatever dark force there is the  audience wants to see their hero lay  into it pretty pretty intensely  and then  we found there was a third law and that  is that you must taste blood to be a man  you've got to go through this  uh in these horror films this uh  coming of age uh through this this blood  experience  uh now joel and ethan cohen have  mentioned that a fourth law exists  that the dead must walk but we're still  uh  we're still testing that law in our  research labs to see if it holds true in  all cases on the one side it is an  action movie and visually very rich and  on the other side it's a love story how  did you balance this  well i tried to  always be true to the character of peter  parker  and  deal with him honestly like in any  dramatic motion picture sometimes in the  comic book movies  they take a step back from reality  even when dealing with the characters  but i decided to take a different  approach and  make it as real as possible so that the  audience believed that they could  believe in this hero in this man  and  that was really my guide all the way  through the performance pieces my advice  to a young filmmaker is to make a movie  every week right every night  and every weekend shoot for two days and  work with actors work with a little 1000  watt lighting kit set it up set up your  shots get a tripod  shoot a little scene work with the  actors cut those scenes together and  then the next weekend  have worked on it with sound and looping  and put some music to it it can all be  done for nothing nowadays with the  computer  and get a response and get a response  from the audience and see where it's  slow and where it doesn't work and your  ideas weren't being communicated  properly learn from that experience  sitting in with the crowd then go out  make another picture the next weekend  and just keep doing it make films no  matter what anybody says and you'll be a  filmmaker it's wonderful to have a  villain in the second picture we you've  got can see his face see their face  emoting expressing  whatever they're feeling  alfred's a great actor and we really  needed somebody with that strong sense  of um reality and somebody with a sense  of humor which alfred has this character  doc ock the way that alfred is creating  him  um  he's really uh a human being a  misunderstood  man  who becomes a beast so what are like  three tips you think for like the good  harp um  let's see  keep in suspense um  deliver the scares  and try and delve into  the unknown things that really scare you  and bring a little bit of that forth  into the picture first of all we've seen  an influx of  the man versus nature films but  generally they're done in a sort of  cheesy tongue-in-cheek way yeah this is  not  what was the  basis to to take it seriously to  actually make it scary again  should we go yes i think alex recognized  in the rasmussen brothers script  that the real power lied in the fact  that it really could happen and so he  took a very realistic approach to his  cgi effects to the performances that he  directed to  everything that he did and um  that's i think why the film is so  effective is  that it doesn't exaggerate it's not  about a giant creature that couldn't  exist it really is a set of  circumstances that could  and i think that's what makes it so  scary i forget you are a big fan of the  marvel comics spider-man how did you  feel when you knew you'd be making a  film  i was absolutely terrified  i got a call  and they told me i was going to be  directing the film  and uh i had no idea how i was ever  going to do that  they had a start date  the script was in not great shape and i  had no idea how i was ever going to  bring off  what i thought all the fans must have  been expecting this great aerial ballet  this graceful movement of spider-man  swooping to the city of manhattan  through the canyons and  it had to be done with great grace and  agility  his movements had to be incredibly fluid  and beautiful to look at  i just didn't know i didn't have a clue  how we're gonna do it it's never easy to  do the right thing so that's why i like  the spider-man movie that we're making  now because it's um  it's about  choices and it shows  not like a message movie but it shows  how we can all make these choices how  will he weigh his  personal needs  against his sense of responsibility  you

